are short of ticks too, and taking the 13% from them lost more than the gate won. So the gate step got\n   cheaper instead. Byte matches in the window come from one equality matrix multiplied along its diagonals rather than\n   hash comparisons per order; window constants are built once; a stage multiplies by a reciprocal rather than dividing a\n   row.\n3. Only four in-window stages remain: orders 3 and 6, and the two word contexts. Removing orders 1, 2, 4 and 16 cost\n   nothing measurable and pays for more gate steps. The word-prefix stage is the one that matters (removing it costs 0.005).\n4. The gate tables' AdaGrad rate is 0.15, within noise of 0.1 and kept for the small trend in its favour.\n\nMeasured and dropped: word-context tables in the softmax, as a swap or an addition (they cost more in ticks and rows than\nthey gave); an order-16 table; thinner rows at order 6 or at low orders; wider low orders; batches of 256 and 512; 48\nhidden units; training the back-off on log p(target) alone, which is far cheaper and much worse.\n\"\"\"'''\ns=s[:a]+doc+s[b:]\nopen('claims/2/train.py','w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"^LOCAL\\|^GATE_TAB\\|^GATE_SHARE\\|^EMBED\\|^ROWS\\|print\" claims/2/train.py | cut -c1-120; python verify.py claims/2/train.py --split dev --seeds 8 --jobs 4 --out runs/claim2.json", "description": "Build second claim and score it on 8 fresh seeds", "run_in_background": true, "timeout": 420000}}}
