{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cgem9sZ8akYZdnyzTRDPZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I submitted two claims that both beat the record on the dev split, each scored on 8 fresh seeds. Claim 2 is my best entry, at 1.0909 bpb against the record's 1.0950. Both ran with no void seeds, and the claim files match the scored runs by hash.\n\n| Claim | Dev bpb (8 fresh seeds) | Seed SD | 95% CI | Gain over 1.0950 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `claims/1` | 1.0913 | 0.0007 | ±0.0006 | 0.0037 |\n| `claims/2` | 1.0909 | 0.0006 | ±0.0005 | 0.0041 |\n\nI used only two of the three claim slots. About 5 minutes remained after claim 2, not enough to find and properly score a third entry.\n\n**What changed from the record** (same architecture):\n- **High-order tables:** orders 8, 10 and 12 go from 8 to 4 floats per row with twice the rows. That halves their backward cost and reduces collisions.\n- **Cheaper gate step:** in-window byte matches now come from one equality matrix multiplied along its diagonals, instead of hash comparisons per order. Window constants are also built once per fit. Together these give about 30% more gate steps on the data-starved gate.\n- **Claim 1:** drops the order 1 and order 16 in-window stages.\n- **Claim 2:** keeps only four stages (orders 3 and 6 plus two word contexts) and raises the gate tables' AdaGrad rate to 0.15.\n\n**How far to trust these numbers:**\n- **Comparison with the record:** the gains compare against the record's stated 8-seed mean, not a same-seed rerun. On seeds 11 and 12, where I ran both directly, the record scored 1.0939 and claim 2's code scored 1.0906. The gain is similar, about 0.0033.\n- **Possible optimism:** the 8 scoring seeds played no part in selection, but the designs were chosen on the same dev bytes, mostly on seeds 11 and 12. Many of those choices came from an offline harness run on seed 11 alone. Dev scores may be slightly optimistic, and the sealed split could sit at a different level.\n- **Claim 2 vs claim 1:** the 0.0004 difference is within noise, so claim 2 is not shown to be better than claim 1. Its `claim.json` says so.\n- **Against the record's own test:** `verify.py` requires a new record to clear a 0.002 margin at p < 0.01. Both gains are above that margin on dev, but only the sealed rerun can settle it.\n- **Tuning left undone:** I never tested the remaining table-phase ideas, such as a shorter full-backward phase, sampling the order-5 backward, and a wider hidden layer. Gate tuning itself looks nearly used up, as most late changes were within noise.\n\nAbout 30 variants were tried and dropped, including word-context tables in the softmax, an order-16 table, other row widths, larger batches and a target-only gate objective. The main ones are listed in the `train.py` docstrings, and the run logs are in `runs/`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1903, "cache_read_input_tokens": 156179, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1903}, "output_tokens": 7, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "507db3bc-24cf-4b4c-ac88-8eb6bce6499d", "uuid": "6e958ced-db57-4442-9724-b1a751d08359", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T20:51:03.435Z", "request_id": "req_011Cgem9sGmcTZNM24rbpz8R"}
